Tip: A bounded numerator over a growing denominator tends to $0$.', '0', '{}'::text[], '$0$', 'limit sin x over x at infinity squeeze theorem'),
  ('calc-limits/x-sin-one-over-x-at-infinity', 'calc-limits', 44, '$\lim_{x \to \infty} x \sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) = \,?$', '$1$

**Substitute** $t = \frac{1}{x}$, so $t \to 0^+$ and the expression becomes $\frac{\sin t}{t} \to 1$. Contrast $\lim_{x \to 0} x \sin\left(\frac{1}{x}\right) = 0$, by the squeeze theorem.

Tip: Substituting $t = \frac{1}{x}$ turns a limit at infinity into a limit at $0$.', '1', '{}'::text[], '$1$', 'limit x sin(1/x) as x approaches infinity substitution'),
  ('calc-limits/sin-x-squared-over-x', 'calc-limits', 45, '$\lim_{x \to 0} \dfrac{\sin(x^2)}{x} = \,?$', '$0$

