  ('calc-series/basel-problem', 'calc-series', 26, '$\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n^2} = \,?$', '$\frac{\pi^2}{6}$

Euler proved this in 1735 (the Basel problem). The partial sums reach about $1.645$.

Watch out: the integral test shows that this series converges, but not what it converges to.', 'pi^2/6', array['(pi^2)/6']::text[], '$\frac{\pi^2}{6}$', 'Basel problem sum 1/n^2 equals pi^2/6'),
  ('calc-series/integral-test-n-ln-n', 'calc-series', 27, 'Does $\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n \ln n}$ converge or diverge?', 'Diverges

The function $\frac{1}{x \ln x}$ is positive, continuous and decreasing for $x \ge 2$. With $u = \ln x$, $\int_2^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x\ln x} = \ln(\ln x)\Big|_2^{\infty} = \infty$.

Key idea: this series diverges even more slowly than the harmonic series.', 'diverges', array['diverge', 'divergent', 'it diverges']::text[], null, 'integral test 1/(n ln n) series diverges'),
  ('calc-series/integral-test-n-ln-squared', 'calc-series', 28, 'Does $\sum_{n=2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n(\ln n)^2}$ converge or diverge?', 'Converges

The function is positive, continuous and decreasing for $x \ge 2$. With $u = \ln x$, $\int_2^{\infty} \frac{dx}{x(\ln x)^2} = \left[-\frac{1}{\ln x}\right]_2^{\infty} = \frac{1}{\ln 2}$, which is finite.

Tip: compare with the previous card. A single extra power of $\ln n$ in the denominator flips the verdict.', 'converges', array['converge', 'convergent', 'it converges']::text[], null, 'integral test 1/(n (ln n)^2) converges'),
  ('calc-series/integral-test-n-e-to-minus-n-squared', 'calc-series', 29, 'Does $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n e^{-n^2}$ converge or diverge?', 'Converges

