  ('sw-data-structures/floyd-cycle-detection', 'sw-data-structures', 41, 'How does Floyd''s tortoise-and-hare algorithm detect a cycle in a linked list, and how much extra space does it use?', 'Advance a slow pointer $1$ node and a fast pointer $2$ nodes per step; if they ever meet, there''s a cycle.

If the fast one reaches the end, there''s no cycle. Inside a cycle the fast pointer gains one node per step on the slow one, so they must meet.

Key idea: it takes $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ extra space, versus $O(n)$ space for a set of visited nodes.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Floyd tortoise and hare cycle detection linked list'),
  ('sw-data-structures/floyd-cycle-start', 'sw-data-structures', 42, 'After Floyd''s tortoise and hare meet inside a linked-list cycle, how do you find the node where the cycle begins?', 'Move one pointer back to the head, then advance both one node at a time; they meet at the cycle''s first node.

Why: if the cycle starts $\mu$ nodes from the head, the meeting point is exactly $\mu$ steps (mod the cycle length) before the cycle start, so both pointers arrive there together.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Floyd cycle detection find start of cycle'),
  ('sw-data-structures/skip-list', 'sw-data-structures', 43, 'Skip list with $n$ elements: expected search time, and how is each node''s number of levels chosen?', 'Expected $O(\log n)$ for search, insert and delete.

A new node''s levels come from coin flips: it''s promoted one more level with probability $1/2$, so level $i$ holds about $n/2^i$ nodes.

Key idea: it''s a randomized alternative to balanced BSTs (Redis sorted sets, Java''s `ConcurrentSkipListMap`).
