  ('sw-data-structures/monotonic-stack-next-greater', 'sw-data-structures', 50, 'Which technique finds the next greater element for every item of an array in $O(n)$ total time?', 'A monotonic stack.

Scan left to right, keeping a stack of indices whose values never increase from bottom to top. Each new value pops every smaller value (it is their **next greater element**), then is pushed.

Key idea: each index is pushed and popped at most once, so the total is $O(n)$.', 'monotonic stack', array['monotone stack', 'monotonic decreasing stack', 'decreasing stack', 'stack']::text[], null, 'monotonic stack next greater element O(n)'),
  ('sw-data-structures/sliding-window-max-deque', 'sw-data-structures', 51, 'To get the maximum of every length-$k$ window of an $n$-element array in $O(n)$ total, which data structure holds the candidates?', 'A monotonic deque of indices whose values decrease from front to back.

For each new element, pop smaller values off the back, push it, and drop the front index once it leaves the window. The front is always the current max.

Key idea: each index enters and leaves once, so it''s $O(n)$, versus $O(nk)$ by brute force.', 'monotonic deque', array['deque', 'monotone deque', 'monotonic queue', 'decreasing deque', 'double-ended queue']::text[], null, 'sliding window maximum monotonic deque O(n)'),
  ('sw-data-structures/hash-table-no-order', 'sw-data-structures', 52, 'Why is a hash table a poor fit when you need the smallest key or every key in a range, and what should you use instead?', 'Hashing scatters keys with no order, so the minimum, a successor or a range query means scanning all $n$ entries.

Tip: use a balanced BST (Java''s `TreeMap`, C++''s `std::map`). It finds the min or a successor in $O(\log n)$ and reports the $k$ keys in a range in $O(\log n + k)$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'hash table no ordering range query minimum balanced BST'),
  ('sw-data-structures/mutable-hash-keys', 'sw-data-structures', 53, 'Why is it a bug to mutate an object while it''s being used as a key in a hash map?', 'The entry sits in the bucket chosen by the key''s old hash.

If you change a field that feeds the hash, lookups go to a different bucket, so the entry is effectively lost.

