Equivalently, $80.0 \times (1/2)^3 = 10.0\,\mathrm{g}$.', '10.0', array['10 g']::text[], null, 'radioactive decay half-life remaining amount after three half-lives'),
  ('chem-thermo-kinetics/zero-order-half-life-calculation', 'chem-thermo-kinetics', 69, 'A zero-order reaction has $[\ce{A}]_0 = 0.80\,\mathrm{M}$ and $k = 0.020\,\mathrm{M/s}$. What is its half-life in seconds?', '20

For zero order, $t_{1/2} = \dfrac{[\ce{A}]_0}{2k} = \dfrac{0.80}{2(0.020)}$.

The half-life of a zero-order reaction is **proportional** to $[\ce{A}]_0$: it shrinks as the reaction goes on.

Watch out: don''t use $\ln 2/k$; that is first order only.', '20', array['20 s']::text[], null, 'zero order half-life formula A0 over 2k calculation'),
  ('chem-thermo-kinetics/second-order-half-life-calculation', 'chem-thermo-kinetics', 70, 'A second-order reaction has $k = 0.50\,\mathrm{M^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ and $[\ce{A}]_0 = 0.20\,\mathrm{M}$. What is its half-life in seconds?', '10

$t_{1/2} = \dfrac{1}{k[\ce{A}]_0} = \dfrac{1}{(0.50)(0.20)} = 10\,\mathrm{s}$.

The half-life of a second-order reaction **grows** as the reaction proceeds, because $[\ce{A}]$ falls.', '10', array['10 s']::text[], null, 'second order half-life formula 1 over k A0 calculation'),
  ('chem-thermo-kinetics/second-order-integrated-law', 'chem-thermo-kinetics', 71, 'Write the integrated rate law for a reaction that is second order in A.', '$\dfrac{1}{[\ce{A}]_t} = kt + \dfrac{1}{[\ce{A}]_0}$

A plot of $1/[\ce{A}]$ against $t$ is a straight line with slope $+k$.

Tip: unlike first order, the line **rises** because $1/[\ce{A}]$ grows as A is consumed.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'second order integrated rate law 1/[A] versus time'),
  ('chem-thermo-kinetics/zero-order-integrated-law', 'chem-thermo-kinetics', 72, 'Write the integrated rate law for a zero-order reaction, and say when zero-order behavior occurs.', '$[\ce{A}]_t = [\ce{A}]_0 - kt$

