Why: it''s a sum of $r$ independent geometric waits, each with mean $\frac{1}{p} = 6$.', '12', array['twelve']::text[], null, 'negative binomial expected rolls for two sixes'),
  ('stats-distributions/poisson-pmf-formula', 'stats-distributions', 39, 'Write the Poisson probability $P(X = k)$ for a count with mean $\lambda$.', '$P(X = k) = \dfrac{e^{-\lambda}\lambda^k}{k!}$, for $k = 0, 1, 2, \ldots$

Unlike the binomial, there''s no upper limit on $k$.

Key idea: both the mean and the variance equal $\lambda$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Poisson probability mass function formula'),
  ('stats-distributions/poisson-zero', 'stats-distributions', 40, 'A Poisson count has mean $\lambda = 2$. What is $P(X = 0)$? Round to 4 decimal places.', '0.1353

$P(X = 0) = \dfrac{e^{-2}2^0}{0!} = e^{-2} \approx 0.1353$

Tip: in general, the chance of no events is $e^{-\lambda}$.', '0.1353', array['e^-2', 'e^(-2)', '13.53%']::text[], '$e^{-2} \approx 0.1353$', 'Poisson probability of zero events e to the minus lambda'),
  ('stats-distributions/poisson-exact-count', 'stats-distributions', 41, 'A Poisson count has mean $\lambda = 3$. What is $P(X = 2)$? Round to 4 decimal places.', '0.2240

$P(X = 2) = \dfrac{e^{-3}3^2}{2!} = 4.5e^{-3} \approx 0.2240$

Watch out: don''t forget the $k!$ in the denominator.', '0.2240', array['22.4%']::text[], null, 'Poisson probability exactly 2 events lambda 3'),
  ('stats-distributions/poisson-rescale-rate', 'stats-distributions', 42, 'Emails arrive as a Poisson process averaging 12 per hour. What is the mean number of emails in a $15\,\mathrm{min}$ window?', '3

