One degree of freedom is used up by estimating $\mu$ with $\bar{x}$.

Watch out: this result underlies intervals and tests for $\sigma^2$, which, unlike $t$ procedures, are very sensitive to non-normal data.', 'n-1', array['df=n-1', 'n minus 1', 'n minus one']::text[], '$n - 1$', '(n-1)s^2 over sigma^2 chi-square degrees of freedom'),
  ('stats-distributions/f-distribution', 'stats-distributions', 94, 'What is an $F$ distribution, and where is it used?', 'The ratio of two independent chi-squares, each divided by its df: $F = \dfrac{U/d_1}{V/d_2}$, with $(d_1, d_2)$ degrees of freedom.

It''s nonnegative and right-skewed.

Tip: it''s used in ANOVA and to compare two variances with $F = \frac{s_1^2}{s_2^2}$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'F distribution definition ratio of chi-squares ANOVA'),
  ('stats-distributions/identify-poisson', 'stats-distributions', 95, 'Typos occur independently at a steady average rate. Which distribution best models the number of typos on a page?', 'Poisson

It counts events in a fixed interval (here, a page) with no fixed upper limit, at a constant average rate $\lambda$.

Tip: estimate $\lambda$ by the mean number of typos per page.', 'Poisson', array['Poisson distribution']::text[], null, 'which distribution typos per page Poisson'),
  ('stats-distributions/identify-geometric', 'stats-distributions', 96, 'Each applicant is qualified with probability $0.3$, independently. Which distribution models the number of interviews up to and including the first qualified applicant?', 'Geometric, with $p = 0.3$ and mean $\frac{1}{0.3} \approx 3.33$ interviews.

You count trials until the first success, so the number of trials isn''t fixed and the binomial doesn''t apply.', 'geometric', array['geometric distribution']::text[], null, 'which distribution trials until first qualified applicant geometric'),
  ('stats-distributions/identify-uniform', 'stats-distributions', 97, 'A bus comes exactly every $15\,\mathrm{min}$, and you arrive at a random time. Which distribution describes your wait?', 'Uniform on $[0, 15]$ minutes

Every point in the cycle is equally likely, so the density is flat at $\frac{1}{15}$ and the mean wait is $7.5\,\mathrm{min}$.

Watch out: if buses instead arrived as a Poisson process, the wait would be exponential.', 'uniform', array['uniform distribution', 'continuous uniform']::text[], null, 'bus every 15 minutes random arrival wait uniform'),
  ('stats-distributions/identify-binomial', 'stats-distributions', 98, '3% of chips in a large production run are defective. Which distribution models the number of defective chips in a random sample of 20?', 'Binomial, with $n = 20$ and $p = 0.03$ (mean $np = 0.6$).

There''s a fixed number of trials with the same defect rate, and the run is large enough (10% condition) that the draws are nearly independent.', 'binomial', array['binomial distribution']::text[], null, 'defective chips sample of 20 binomial distribution'),
  ('stats-distributions/poisson-normal-approximation', 'stats-distributions', 99, 'For a large mean $\lambda$, which continuous distribution approximates a Poisson count?', 'A normal distribution with mean $\lambda$ and SD $\sqrt{\lambda}$

A Poisson with a whole-number mean $\lambda$ is a sum of $\lambda$ independent $\text{Poisson}(1)$ counts, so the CLT applies.

Tip: the approximation improves as $\lambda$ grows.', 'normal', array['normal distribution', 'Gaussian']::text[], null, 'normal approximation to Poisson large lambda');
insert into pack_card_rows values -- stats-inference
  ('stats-inference/null-vs-alternative', 'stats-inference', 0, 'What do $H_0$ and $H_a$ represent, and which one contains the equals sign?', '$H_0$ is the null (it holds the equality); $H_a$ is the alternative.

The **null hypothesis** is the skeptical "no effect, no difference" claim, e.g. $H_0: \mu = 50$. The **alternative hypothesis** is what the study seeks evidence for: $\mu \ne 50$, $\mu < 50$ or $\mu > 50$.

Watch out: both are statements about parameters ($\mu$, $p$), never sample statistics ($\bar{x}$, $\hat{p}$).
Tip: write $H_0$ and $H_a$ before looking at the data.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'null hypothesis alternative hypothesis equals sign'),
  ('stats-inference/p-value-definition', 'stats-inference', 1, 'What exactly is a p-value?', 'The probability, assuming $H_0$ is true, of a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed.

"Extreme" means in the direction of $H_a$: one tail for a one-sided test, both tails for a two-sided test. A small **p-value** means the data would be surprising if $H_0$ were true.

Watch out: it is not the probability that $H_0$ is true, and not the probability that the result is due to chance alone.
Key idea: the p-value measures how well the data fit $H_0$, not how likely $H_0$ is.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'p-value definition probability assuming null true'),
  ('stats-inference/fail-to-reject', 'stats-inference', 2, 'A test at $\alpha = 0.05$ gives $p = 0.08$. What is the correct conclusion?', 'Fail to reject $H_0$: the data do not provide convincing evidence for $H_a$.

Since $p = 0.08 > \alpha = 0.05$, the result is not statistically significant at the $5\%$ level, so we **fail to reject** $H_0$.

Watch out: never write "accept $H_0$" or "$H_0$ is true". A large p-value means insufficient evidence against $H_0$, not proof of it.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'fail to reject null hypothesis p value greater than alpha'),
  ('stats-inference/type-i-error', 'stats-inference', 3, 'Rejecting $H_0$ when $H_0$ is actually true is which type of error?', 'Type I error, a false positive.

Its probability is $\alpha$: at $\alpha = 0.05$, a true $H_0$ is wrongly rejected $5\%$ of the time. A **Type II error** is failing to reject a false $H_0$; its probability is $\beta$.

Mnemonic: Type I is the false alarm, like convicting an innocent defendant.', 'Type I', array['type 1', 'type-I', 'type-1', 'type one', 'I', '1', 'type I error', 'type 1 error', 'type one error', 'false positive']::text[], null, 'Type I error false positive reject true null'),
  ('stats-inference/power', 'stats-inference', 4, 'A test has $P(\text{Type II error}) = \beta = 0.2$. What is its power?', '$0.8$.

**Power** $= 1 - \beta = P(\text{reject } H_0 \mid H_0 \text{ false})$: the chance of detecting a real effect.

Tip: power rises with a larger $n$, a larger true effect, a larger $\alpha$ or less variability. Increasing $n$ is usually the practical lever.', '0.8', array['80%', '4/5']::text[], null, 'statistical power 1 minus beta Type II error'),
  ('stats-inference/margin-of-error-95', 'stats-inference', 5, 'With known $\sigma = 12$ and $n = 36$, what is the margin of error of a $95\%$ confidence interval for $\mu$?', '$3.92$.

$\mathrm{ME} = z^*\dfrac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}} = 1.96 \times \dfrac{12}{\sqrt{36}} = 1.96 \times 2 = 3.92$, so the **confidence interval** is $\bar{x} \pm 3.92$.

Tip: other levels use $z^* = 1.645$ for $90\%$ and $z^* = 2.576$ for $99\%$.', '3.92', '{}'::text[], null, 'margin of error 95% confidence interval known sigma'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-interpretation', 'stats-inference', 6, 'What does the ''$95\%$'' in a $95\%$ confidence interval actually mean?', 'The method''s long-run **success rate**.

If we took many random samples and built a $95\%$ CI from each, about $95\%$ of those intervals would contain the true parameter.

Watch out: it does not mean "a $95\%$ chance $\mu$ is in this interval" ($\mu$ is fixed; this interval either contains it or not), nor that $95\%$ of the data fall in it.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'interpret 95% confidence interval meaning'),
  ('stats-inference/halve-margin-of-error', 'stats-inference', 7, 'To cut a confidence interval''s margin of error in half (same confidence level), multiply the sample size by what?', '$4$.

$\mathrm{ME} = z^*\dfrac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}$ is proportional to $\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{n}}$, so halving it means doubling $\sqrt{n}$, which takes **four times** as many observations.

Example: going from $n = 100$ to $n = 400$ cuts the margin of error in half.
Watch out: merely doubling $n$ shrinks the margin of error by a factor of only $\sqrt{2}$.', '4', array['four', '4 times', 'four times', '4x', 'x4', 'times 4', 'quadruple']::text[], null, 'halve margin of error sample size four times'),
  ('stats-inference/paired-vs-two-sample', 'stats-inference', 8, 'Blood pressure is measured on the same $20$ patients before and after a drug. Which $t$-test fits: paired or two-sample?', 'Paired.

Each patient gives two linked measurements, so take the **differences** (after minus before) and run a one-sample $t$-test on their mean, with $\mathrm{df} = 20 - 1 = 19$.

Watch out: a two-sample $t$-test is for two independent groups. Using it on paired data ignores the pairing and throws away power.', 'paired', array['paired t-test', 'paired t test', 'paired t', 'paired test', 'matched pairs', 'matched pairs t-test', 'matched-pairs', 'matched pair', 'paired samples']::text[], null, 'paired t-test versus two-sample t-test before after'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-square-df', 'stats-inference', 9, 'A chi-square test of independence uses a table with $3$ rows and $4$ columns. How many degrees of freedom?', '$6$.

$\mathrm{df} = (r-1)(c-1) = (3-1)(4-1) = 6$.

Watch out: do not count the totals row or column.
Tip: each expected count is $\dfrac{\text{row total} \times \text{column total}}{\text{table total}}$, and every expected count should be at least $5$.', '6', array['df=6', '6 df', 'six']::text[], null, 'chi-square test of independence degrees of freedom'),
  ('stats-inference/correlation-range', 'stats-inference', 10, 'What values can the correlation coefficient $r$ take, and what does $r = -0.9$ indicate?', 'Any value from $-1$ to $1$; $r = -0.9$ is a strong negative linear association.

The sign gives the direction and the size gives the strength of the **linear** association. $r$ has no units and does not change if you swap $x$ and $y$ or change units.

Watch out: $r \approx 0$ can hide a strong curved pattern, because $r$ measures only linear association.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'correlation coefficient r range strength direction'),
  ('stats-inference/correlation-not-causation', 'stats-inference', 11, 'Ice cream sales and drowning deaths are strongly correlated. Why can''t we conclude ice cream causes drowning?', 'Correlation does not imply causation.

A **lurking (confounding) variable**, hot weather, drives both ice cream sales and swimming, and so drowning risk.

Key idea: only a randomized experiment can establish cause and effect; observational data like these show association only.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'correlation does not imply causation confounding variable'),
  ('stats-inference/least-squares-slope', 'stats-inference', 12, '$r = 0.8$, $s_x = 5$, and $s_y = 10$. What is the least-squares slope $b_1$ for predicting $y$ from $x$?', '$1.6$.

$b_1 = r\dfrac{s_y}{s_x} = 0.8 \times \dfrac{10}{5} = 1.6$. Each $1$-unit increase in $x$ predicts a $1.6$-unit increase in $y$, on average.

Tip: the **slope** always has the same sign as $r$.', '1.6', array['8/5']::text[], null, 'least squares slope r times sy over sx'),
  ('stats-inference/least-squares-intercept', 'stats-inference', 13, 'A least-squares line has slope $b_1 = 3$, and the data have $\bar{x} = 10$ and $\bar{y} = 50$. What is the intercept $b_0$?', '$20$.

The least-squares line always passes through $(\bar{x}, \bar{y})$, so $b_0 = \bar{y} - b_1\bar{x} = 50 - 3(10) = 20$, giving $\hat{y} = 20 + 3x$.

Tip: if you know $b_1$, $\bar{x}$ and $\bar{y}$, the **intercept** takes one line of arithmetic.', '20', array['b0 = 20']::text[], null, 'least squares intercept y bar minus slope x bar'),
  ('stats-inference/residual', 'stats-inference', 14, 'Using $\hat{y} = 20 + 3x$, a data point has $x = 10$ and observed $y = 47$. What is its residual?', '$-3$.

**Residual** $=$ observed $-$ predicted: $e = y - \hat{y} = 47 - (20 + 30) = -3$.

Watch out: a negative residual means the point lies below the line (the model overestimated it).
Key idea: least squares minimizes the sum of the squared residuals.', '-3', '{}'::text[], '$-3$', 'residual observed minus predicted regression'),
  ('stats-inference/r-squared', 'stats-inference', 15, 'A linear regression has $r = 0.6$. What fraction of the variation in $y$ is explained by the model?', '$0.36$, i.e. $36\%$.

$R^2 = r^2 = 0.6^2 = 0.36$: $36\%$ of the variability in $y$ is accounted for by the linear relationship with $x$.

Watch out: it does not mean that $36\%$ of the points fall on the line.', '0.36', array['36%', '9/25']::text[], null, 'r squared coefficient of determination explained variation'),
  ('stats-inference/point-estimate-definition', 'stats-inference', 16, 'What is a point estimate, and how does it differ from an interval estimate?', 'A point estimate is a single number computed from the sample (such as $\bar{x}$ or $\hat{p}$) that estimates a parameter.

An **interval estimate** adds a margin of error to say how precise that single number is: $\text{estimate} \pm \text{margin of error}$.

Key idea: a point estimate almost never equals the parameter exactly, which is why we report a confidence interval alongside it.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'point estimate versus interval estimate confidence interval'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-formula-z', 'stats-inference', 17, 'What is the formula for a confidence interval for $\mu$ when $\sigma$ is known?', '$\bar{x} \pm z^*\dfrac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}$

The **critical value** $z^*$ sets the confidence level, and $\dfrac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of $\bar{x}$.

Tip: in practice $\sigma$ is rarely known, so the $t$ interval $\bar{x} \pm t^*\dfrac{s}{\sqrt{n}}$ is used far more often.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'confidence interval mean known sigma formula z critical value'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-margin-of-error-factors', 'stats-inference', 18, 'What three things determine the margin of error of a confidence interval for a mean, and in which direction does each push it?', 'A higher confidence level and a larger $\sigma$ widen it; a larger $n$ narrows it.

In $\mathrm{ME} = z^*\dfrac{\sigma}{\sqrt{n}}$, a higher confidence level means a larger $z^*$, more variability means a larger $\sigma$, and a larger sample size shrinks the **standard error**.

Watch out: the margin of error accounts only for sampling variability, not for bias from a poorly designed sample.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'margin of error factors confidence level variability sample size'),
  ('stats-inference/se-proportion', 'stats-inference', 19, 'What is the standard deviation of $\hat{p}$ when $p = 0.5$ and $n = 100$?', '$0.05$.

$\sqrt{\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}} = \sqrt{\dfrac{0.5 \times 0.5}{100}} = \sqrt{0.0025} = 0.05$.

Tip: for a confidence interval, use $\hat{p}$ in place of $p$ to get the **standard error**; for a test of $H_0: p = p_0$, use $p_0$.', '0.05', array['5/100', '1/20']::text[], null, 'standard deviation of sample proportion p hat formula'),
  ('stats-inference/z-star-90', 'stats-inference', 20, 'What is the critical value $z^*$ for a $90\%$ confidence interval? Use the standard table value.', '$1.645$.

The middle $90\%$ of a standard normal lies between $-1.645$ and $1.645$, leaving $5\%$ in each tail.

Tip: memorize $1.645$, $1.96$ and $2.576$ for $90\%$, $95\%$ and $99\%$ confidence.', '1.645', array['1.64', '1.65']::text[], null, 'z critical value 90 percent confidence interval 1.645'),
  ('stats-inference/z-star-95', 'stats-inference', 21, 'What critical value $z^*$ gives a $95\%$ confidence interval for a mean when $\sigma$ is known?', '$1.96$.

The middle $95\%$ of a standard normal lies between $-1.96$ and $1.96$, leaving $2.5\%$ in each tail.

Tip: back-of-envelope intervals round $z^*$ to $2$, which is why "estimate $\pm$ two standard errors" is a popular rule of thumb.', '1.96', '{}'::text[], null, 'z critical value 95 percent confidence interval 1.96'),
  ('stats-inference/z-star-99', 'stats-inference', 22, 'What is the critical value $z^*$ for a $99\%$ confidence interval? Use the standard table value.', '$2.576$.

The middle $99\%$ of a standard normal lies between $-2.576$ and $2.576$, leaving $0.5\%$ in each tail.

Watch out: a higher confidence level means a larger $z^*$ and a wider interval.', '2.576', array['2.58', '2.575']::text[], null, 'z critical value 99 percent confidence interval 2.576'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-compute-lower-endpoint', 'stats-inference', 23, 'A sample of $n = 25$ has $\bar{x} = 50$ and known $\sigma = 10$. What is the lower endpoint of the $95\%$ confidence interval for $\mu$? Use $z^* = 1.96$.', '$46.08$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \dfrac{10}{\sqrt{25}} = 2$, so $\mathrm{ME} = 1.96 \times 2 = 3.92$ and the interval is $50 \pm 3.92 = (46.08,\ 53.92)$.

Tip: compute the **standard error** first, then multiply by $z^*$, then add and subtract.', '46.08', '{}'::text[], null, 'confidence interval lower endpoint z interval worked example'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-width-vs-confidence', 'stats-inference', 24, 'With the sample size fixed, what happens to the width of a confidence interval when you raise the confidence level from $95\%$ to $99\%$?', 'It gets wider.

A larger critical value ($2.576$ instead of $1.96$) multiplies the same standard error. You buy more confidence by giving up **precision**.

Tip: to gain both confidence and precision, you need a larger sample.', 'wider', array['wide', 'larger', 'bigger', 'increases', 'it gets wider']::text[], null, 'confidence level width wider interval trade off precision'),
  ('stats-inference/sample-size-mean', 'stats-inference', 25, 'How large a sample is needed to estimate $\mu$ within $\pm 3$ at $95\%$ confidence if $\sigma = 15$? Round up to a whole number.', '$97$.

$n = \left(\dfrac{z^*\sigma}{E}\right)^2 = \left(\dfrac{1.96 \times 15}{3}\right)^2 = 9.8^2 = 96.04$.

Watch out: always round **up**. Rounding $96.04$ down to $96$ would miss the target margin of error.', '97', '{}'::text[], null, 'sample size for estimating mean margin of error formula'),
  ('stats-inference/sample-size-proportion', 'stats-inference', 26, 'Using the conservative value $\hat{p} = 0.5$, how large must $n$ be for a $95\%$ confidence interval for a proportion with a margin of error of $0.03$? Round up.', '$1068$.

$n = \left(\dfrac{z^*}{E}\right)^2 \hat{p}(1-\hat{p}) = \left(\dfrac{1.96}{0.03}\right)^2 (0.25) \approx 1067.1$.

Why: $\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})$ is largest at $\hat{p} = 0.5$, so this choice guarantees the target margin of error whatever the true $p$ is.
Watch out: round **up** to $1068$.', '1068', '{}'::text[], null, 'sample size proportion conservative p 0.5 margin of error'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-t-formula', 'stats-inference', 27, 'What is the formula for a $t$ confidence interval for a mean, and how many degrees of freedom does it use?', '$\bar{x} \pm t^*\dfrac{s}{\sqrt{n}}$, with $\mathrm{df} = n - 1$.

Use the sample standard deviation $s$ in the **standard error** $\dfrac{s}{\sqrt{n}}$ and find $t^*$ for the confidence level and $\mathrm{df}$.

Tip: for the same confidence level, $t^*$ is larger than $z^*$, so a $t$ interval is slightly wider.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 't confidence interval mean formula degrees of freedom'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-t-margin-of-error', 'stats-inference', 28, '$\bar{x} = 20$, $s = 5$, $n = 16$. What is the margin of error of a $95\%$ $t$ interval? Use $t^* = 2.131$ and round to 2 decimals.', '$2.66$.

$\mathrm{ME} = t^*\dfrac{s}{\sqrt{n}} = 2.131 \times \dfrac{5}{4} = 2.664$, so the CI is $20 \pm 2.66 = (17.34,\ 22.66)$.

Watch out: $t^* = 2.131$ comes from $\mathrm{df} = 16 - 1 = 15$, not from $n$.', '2.66', array['2.664']::text[], null, 't interval margin of error worked example sample standard deviation'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-conditions-mean', 'stats-inference', 29, 'What conditions should hold before you use a $t$ interval for a mean?', 'The sample is random, the observations are independent, and the data are approximately normal (or $n$ is large).

- **Random**: a random sample or randomized experiment.
- **Independent**: when sampling without replacement, the sample is under $10\%$ of the population.
- **Normal**: the population is roughly normal, or $n \ge 30$. For a small sample, check a plot for strong skew and outliers.

Watch out: a large sample does not repair a biased sampling method.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'conditions for t interval random independent normal 10 percent'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-proportion-margin-of-error', 'stats-inference', 30, '$\hat{p} = 0.4$ from a sample of $n = 100$. What is the margin of error of a $95\%$ confidence interval for $p$? Round to 3 decimals.', '$0.096$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \sqrt{\dfrac{0.4 \times 0.6}{100}} \approx 0.049$, so $\mathrm{ME} = 1.96 \times 0.049 \approx 0.096$.

Tip: the interval is $0.4 \pm 0.096 = (0.304,\ 0.496)$.', '0.096', '{}'::text[], null, 'proportion confidence interval margin of error worked example'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-proportion-conditions', 'stats-inference', 31, 'What is the success-failure condition for a confidence interval for a proportion?', '$n\hat{p} \ge 10$ and $n(1-\hat{p}) \ge 10$.

It guarantees that the sampling distribution of $\hat{p}$ is close enough to **normal** for $z$ methods to work. The sample must also be random, with the observations independent.

Watch out: for a test of $H_0: p = p_0$, check $np_0 \ge 10$ and $n(1-p_0) \ge 10$ instead.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'success failure condition proportion confidence interval np at least 10'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-test-duality', 'stats-inference', 32, 'A $95\%$ CI for $\mu$ is $(12, 18)$. Would a two-sided test of $H_0: \mu = 20$ at $\alpha = 0.05$ reject $H_0$? Answer yes or no.', 'Yes.

The value $20$ lies outside the interval, so it is not a plausible value for $\mu$ at the $95\%$ level, and the two-sided test at $\alpha = 0.05$ **rejects** $H_0$.

Key idea: a $(1-\alpha)$ confidence interval and a two-sided test at level $\alpha$ agree: reject exactly when the null value falls outside the interval.', 'yes', array['y', 'reject', 'yes, reject', 'yes reject']::text[], null, 'confidence interval two-sided hypothesis test equivalence reject null value'),
  ('stats-inference/z-test-statistic', 'stats-inference', 33, 'What is the test statistic for a one-sample $z$ test of $H_0: \mu = \mu_0$ when $\sigma$ is known?', '$z = \dfrac{\bar{x} - \mu_0}{\sigma/\sqrt{n}}$

It counts how many **standard errors** the sample mean lies from the hypothesized mean. Large $|z|$ means the data are far from what $H_0$ predicts.

Tip: every test statistic has the form $\dfrac{\text{estimate} - \text{null value}}{\text{standard error}}$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'one-sample z test statistic formula known sigma'),
  ('stats-inference/z-test-compute', 'stats-inference', 34, '$\bar{x} = 52$, $\mu_0 = 50$, $\sigma = 10$, $n = 25$. What is the $z$ statistic?', '$1$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \dfrac{10}{\sqrt{25}} = 2$, so $z = \dfrac{52 - 50}{2} = 1$.

Watch out: divide by the standard error $\sigma/\sqrt{n}$, not by $\sigma$.', '1', '{}'::text[], null, 'z statistic worked example sample mean hypothesized mean'),
  ('stats-inference/t-test-statistic', 'stats-inference', 35, 'What is the test statistic for a one-sample $t$ test of $H_0: \mu = \mu_0$, and how many degrees of freedom does it have?', '$t = \dfrac{\bar{x} - \mu_0}{s/\sqrt{n}}$, with $\mathrm{df} = n - 1$.

It has the same form as $z$, but uses the sample standard deviation $s$, which adds uncertainty and gives the **$t$ distribution** heavier tails.

Tip: for large $n$ the $t$ and $z$ results are nearly identical.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'one-sample t test statistic formula degrees of freedom'),
  ('stats-inference/t-test-compute', 'stats-inference', 36, '$\bar{x} = 105$, $\mu_0 = 100$, $s = 15$, $n = 36$. What is the $t$ statistic?', '$2$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \dfrac{15}{\sqrt{36}} = 2.5$, so $t = \dfrac{105 - 100}{2.5} = 2$, with $\mathrm{df} = 35$.

Watch out: $\sqrt{36} = 6$; forgetting the square root of $n$ is a classic slip.', '2', '{}'::text[], null, 't statistic worked example one-sample sample standard deviation'),
  ('stats-inference/p-value-two-sided-z', 'stats-inference', 37, 'In a two-sided $z$ test, $z = 1.96$. What is the p-value? Round to 2 decimals.', '$0.05$.

The upper tail beyond $1.96$ has area $0.025$; a two-sided test counts both tails, so $p = 2(0.025) = 0.05$.

Watch out: forgetting to double the tail area is the most common two-sided error.', '0.05', '{}'::text[], null, 'two-sided z test p-value 1.96 both tails'),
  ('stats-inference/p-value-right-tailed-z', 'stats-inference', 38, 'For $H_a: \mu > \mu_0$ the $z$ statistic is $1.645$. What is the p-value? Round to 2 decimals.', '$0.05$.

For a right-tailed test the p-value is just the upper-tail area beyond the statistic: $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05$.

Key idea: one-sided tests use one tail; two-sided tests double it.', '0.05', '{}'::text[], null, 'right-tailed z test p-value 1.645 upper tail'),
  ('stats-inference/critical-value-right-tailed', 'stats-inference', 39, 'To reject $H_0$ in a right-tailed $z$ test at $\alpha = 0.05$, the statistic must exceed what critical value?', '$1.645$.

All $5\%$ of the rejection region sits in the upper tail, so the cutoff is the $95$th percentile of the standard normal.

Tip: the **rejection region** approach and the p-value approach always give the same decision.', '1.645', array['1.64', '1.65']::text[], null, 'critical value right-tailed z test alpha 0.05 rejection region'),
  ('stats-inference/critical-value-two-sided-01', 'stats-inference', 40, 'To reject $H_0$ in a two-sided $z$ test at $\alpha = 0.01$, $|z|$ must exceed what critical value?', '$2.576$.

Split $\alpha$ between the tails: $0.005$ in each, so the cutoffs are $\pm 2.576$.

Tip: the same value appears in a $99\%$ confidence interval, because a $99\%$ interval corresponds to a two-sided test at $\alpha = 0.01$.', '2.576', array['2.58', '2.575']::text[], null, 'two-sided z test critical value alpha 0.01 2.576'),
  ('stats-inference/decision-rule-p-alpha', 'stats-inference', 41, 'How do you decide whether to reject $H_0$ using the p-value and the significance level $\alpha$?', 'Reject $H_0$ when $p \le \alpha$; otherwise fail to reject.

If $p \le \alpha$ the result is **statistically significant**: data this extreme would be rare if $H_0$ were true.

Watch out: choose $\alpha$ before collecting data, not after seeing the p-value.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'hypothesis test decision rule p-value less than alpha reject'),
  ('stats-inference/one-vs-two-sided', 'stats-inference', 42, 'When should you use a one-sided alternative instead of a two-sided one?', 'Only when the research question, stated before seeing the data, cares about a difference in one direction alone.

The default is **two-sided**: $H_a: \mu \ne \mu_0$. A one-sided test puts all of $\alpha$ in one tail, so it is easier to reject in that direction but blind to the other.

Watch out: switching to a one-sided test after seeing which way the data point is cheating.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'one-sided versus two-sided alternative hypothesis when to use'),
  ('stats-inference/statistical-vs-practical', 'stats-inference', 43, 'A huge study finds a statistically significant difference that is tiny in size. What does this illustrate?', 'Statistical significance is not the same as **practical significance**.

With a very large $n$ the standard error is tiny, so even a trivial difference gives a small p-value.

Tip: always report the size of the effect, for instance with a confidence interval, not just the p-value.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'statistical significance versus practical significance large sample'),
  ('stats-inference/type-ii-error', 'stats-inference', 44, 'Failing to reject a false $H_0$ is which type of error?', 'Type II error, a false negative.

Its probability is $\beta$, and power is $1 - \beta$. A **Type I error** is rejecting a true $H_0$.

Mnemonic: Type II is a miss: the effect was real, but the test missed it.', 'Type II', array['type 2', 'type-II', 'type-2', 'type two', 'II', '2', 'type II error', 'type 2 error', 'false negative']::text[], null, 'Type II error false negative fail to reject false null'),
  ('stats-inference/alpha-beta-tradeoff', 'stats-inference', 45, 'With $n$ fixed, you lower $\alpha$ from $0.05$ to $0.01$. Does $\beta$ increase or decrease?', 'It increases, so power falls.

A smaller $\alpha$ makes the rejection region smaller, which makes it harder to reject, even when $H_0$ is false.

Tip: the only way to reduce both error rates at once is to increase $n$.', 'increase', array['increases', 'it increases', 'goes up', 'larger', 'bigger', 'higher']::text[], null, 'alpha beta tradeoff lower significance level type II error increases'),
  ('stats-inference/power-factors', 'stats-inference', 46, 'Which one of these lowers the power of a test: a larger $n$, a larger true effect, or a smaller $\alpha$?', 'A smaller $\alpha$.

A larger $n$ shrinks the standard error and a larger true effect moves the statistic further from $H_0$; both **raise** power. A smaller $\alpha$ makes rejection harder.

Tip: less variability in the data also raises power.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'factors affecting power hypothesis test sample size alpha'),
  ('stats-inference/multiple-testing', 'stats-inference', 47, 'You run $20$ independent tests, each at $\alpha = 0.05$, and every null hypothesis is true. About how many false positives do you expect?', '$1$.

The expected count is $20 \times 0.05 = 1$, and the chance of at least one false positive is $1 - 0.95^{20} \approx 0.64$.

Watch out: running many tests and reporting only the significant ones inflates the **Type I error** rate. Adjust, for example with a Bonferroni correction ($\alpha/20$).', '1', array['one']::text[], null, 'multiple comparisons false positives 20 tests alpha 0.05'),
  ('stats-inference/prop-test-statistic', 'stats-inference', 48, 'What is the test statistic for a one-proportion $z$ test of $H_0: p = p_0$?', '$z = \dfrac{\hat{p} - p_0}{\sqrt{p_0(1-p_0)/n}}$

The **standard error** uses the null value $p_0$, because the test assumes $H_0$ is true.

Watch out: a confidence interval for $p$ uses $\hat{p}$ in its standard error; the test uses $p_0$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'one-proportion z test statistic formula p0'),
  ('stats-inference/prop-test-compute', 'stats-inference', 49, '$H_0: p = 0.5$ with $\hat{p} = 0.56$ and $n = 100$. What is the $z$ statistic?', '$1.2$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \sqrt{\dfrac{0.5 \times 0.5}{100}} = 0.05$, so $z = \dfrac{0.56 - 0.5}{0.05} = 1.2$.

Tip: with $z = 1.2$ the two-sided p-value is about $0.23$, so there is no convincing evidence against $H_0$.', '1.2', '{}'::text[], null, 'one-proportion z test worked example p hat p0'),
  ('stats-inference/conclusion-in-context', 'stats-inference', 50, 'A test of $H_0: p = 0.5$ gives a p-value of $0.03$ at $\alpha = 0.05$. How should you state the conclusion?', 'Reject $H_0$: there is convincing evidence that the true proportion differs from $0.5$.

A good conclusion has three parts: the **decision** (reject or fail to reject), a link to the p-value and $\alpha$, and a statement about the claim in context.

Watch out: do not write that "$H_a$ is proven" or that there is a $3\%$ chance $H_0$ is true.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'hypothesis test conclusion in context reject null p-value 0.03'),
  ('stats-inference/paired-t-compute', 'stats-inference', 51, 'Paired differences have $\bar{d} = 2.5$, $s_d = 5$ and $n = 25$. What is $t$ for $H_0: \mu_d = 0$?', '$2.5$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \dfrac{5}{\sqrt{25}} = 1$, so $t = \dfrac{2.5 - 0}{1} = 2.5$, with $\mathrm{df} = 24$.

Tip: a paired test is just a one-sample $t$ test on the **differences**.', '2.5', '{}'::text[], null, 'paired t test statistic worked example mean difference'),
  ('stats-inference/paired-design-advantage', 'stats-inference', 52, 'Why can a paired design be more powerful than two independent samples?', 'Differences within each pair cancel out person-to-person variability.

Subjects (or twins, or before-and-after measurements) serve as their own **controls**, so the standard error of the mean difference is smaller and real effects are easier to detect.

Watch out: pairing only helps when the paired measurements are positively correlated.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'paired design advantage matched pairs reduces variability'),
  ('stats-inference/bootstrap-idea', 'stats-inference', 53, 'What is the bootstrap, and what does it approximate?', 'Resampling with replacement from the observed sample, many times, to approximate the sampling distribution of a statistic.

Each **resample** has the same size as the original sample; computing the statistic for each gives an estimate of its standard error or a confidence interval.

Tip: it needs no normality assumption, which makes it useful for statistics like the median.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'bootstrap resampling with replacement sampling distribution'),
  ('stats-inference/permutation-test-idea', 'stats-inference', 54, 'How does a permutation test judge whether two groups differ?', 'Shuffle the group labels many times and see how often the shuffled difference is as large as the observed one.

If $H_0$ (the labels do not matter) were true, any shuffle is equally likely. The fraction of shuffles at least as extreme as the observed result is the **p-value**.

Tip: like the bootstrap, it needs no normality assumption.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'permutation test shuffle group labels p-value'),
  ('stats-inference/random-assignment-vs-sampling', 'stats-inference', 55, 'What does random assignment of subjects to treatments allow that random sampling alone does not?', 'Conclusions about **cause and effect**.

Random assignment balances lurking variables across groups on average, so a difference in outcomes can be attributed to the treatment. Random sampling, by contrast, lets you generalize to the population.

Key idea: random sampling supports generalization; random assignment supports causation.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'random assignment versus random sampling cause and effect experiment'),
  ('stats-inference/effect-size-cohens-d', 'stats-inference', 56, 'Two groups means differ by $5$ and the pooled standard deviation is $10$. What is Cohen''s $d$?', '$0.5$.

$d = \dfrac{\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2}{s_p} = \dfrac{5}{10} = 0.5$: the difference measured in standard deviations.

Tip: rough benchmarks are $0.2$ small, $0.5$ medium and $0.8$ large. Unlike a p-value, an **effect size** does not shrink or grow with $n$.', '0.5', array['1/2']::text[], null, 'Cohen''s d effect size difference in means pooled standard deviation'),
  ('stats-inference/two-sample-se', 'stats-inference', 57, 'Two independent samples have $s_1 = 12$, $n_1 = 16$, $s_2 = 16$ and $n_2 = 16$. What is the standard error $\sqrt{\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2}}$ of $\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2$?', '$5$.

$\dfrac{12^2}{16} = 9$ and $\dfrac{16^2}{16} = 16$, so $\mathrm{SE} = \sqrt{9 + 16} = \sqrt{25} = 5$.

Key idea: the **variances** of independent estimates add, so add the squared standard errors and then take the square root.', '5', '{}'::text[], null, 'two-sample t standard error difference of means formula'),
  ('stats-inference/two-sample-t-compute', 'stats-inference', 58, 'Two independent groups give $\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2 = 10$ with standard error $5$. What is the two-sample $t$ statistic for $H_0: \mu_1 = \mu_2$?', '$2$.

$t = \dfrac{(\bar{x}_1 - \bar{x}_2) - 0}{\mathrm{SE}} = \dfrac{10}{5} = 2$.

Tip: the null value for the difference is $0$, so it drops out of the numerator.', '2', '{}'::text[], null, 'two-sample t statistic worked example difference in means'),
  ('stats-inference/two-sample-df-conservative', 'stats-inference', 59, 'For a two-sample $t$ procedure with $n_1 = 12$ and $n_2 = 20$, what is the conservative degrees of freedom, $\min(n_1 - 1,\ n_2 - 1)$?', '$11$.

$\min(12 - 1,\ 20 - 1) = \min(11, 19) = 11$.

Watch out: software uses the more precise **Welch** formula, which gives a non-integer between $11$ and $30$; the minimum is the safe by-hand choice.', '11', '{}'::text[], null, 'two-sample t degrees of freedom conservative minimum n minus 1'),
  ('stats-inference/two-sample-conditions', 'stats-inference', 60, 'What conditions should hold for a two-sample $t$ procedure comparing two means?', 'Two independent random samples (or two randomly assigned groups), each approximately normal or large.

- **Random**: each sample is random, or subjects are randomly assigned.
- **Independent**: the two groups are independent of each other and each is under $10\%$ of its population.
- **Normal**: each population is roughly normal, or each $n \ge 30$.

Watch out: if the measurements are naturally linked in pairs, use a paired test instead.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'two-sample t test conditions independent groups normal'),
  ('stats-inference/ci-difference-contains-zero', 'stats-inference', 61, 'A $95\%$ CI for $\mu_1 - \mu_2$ is $(-1.2,\ 4.6)$. Does a two-sided test of $H_0: \mu_1 = \mu_2$ at $\alpha = 0.05$ reject $H_0$? Answer yes or no.', 'No.

The interval contains $0$, so "no difference" is a plausible value, and the test **fails to reject** $H_0$.

Tip: the interval also shows the plausible size and direction of the difference, which a p-value cannot.', 'no', array['n', 'fail to reject', 'do not reject', 'don''t reject']::text[], null, 'confidence interval difference of means contains zero no significant difference'),
  ('stats-inference/pooled-proportion', 'stats-inference', 62, 'Group 1 has $30$ successes in $n_1 = 100$ and group 2 has $50$ in $n_2 = 150$. What is the pooled proportion $\hat{p}$ for a two-proportion $z$ test?', '$0.32$.

$\hat{p} = \dfrac{30 + 50}{100 + 150} = \dfrac{80}{250} = 0.32$.

Key idea: under $H_0: p_1 = p_2$ there is one common proportion, and the **pooled** estimate uses all of the data.', '0.32', array['8/25']::text[], null, 'pooled proportion two-proportion z test formula'),
  ('stats-inference/pooled-vs-unpooled', 'stats-inference', 63, 'Why does a two-proportion $z$ test pool the samples, while a confidence interval for $p_1 - p_2$ does not?', 'The test assumes $H_0: p_1 = p_2$, so one **common** proportion best describes both groups; the interval makes no such assumption.

A confidence interval estimates the actual difference, so each group keeps its own $\hat{p}_i$ in the standard error.

Tip: the same logic explains why a one-proportion test uses $p_0$ in its standard error while the interval uses $\hat{p}$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'two proportion test pooled versus confidence interval unpooled standard error'),
  ('stats-inference/twins-paired', 'stats-inference', 64, '$25$ pairs of twins are studied; within each pair, one twin is randomly assigned a new treatment and the other a placebo. Which procedure compares the treatments: paired or two-sample?', 'Paired.

The twins in each pair are linked, so analyze the $25$ **differences** with a one-sample $t$ procedure ($\mathrm{df} = 24$).

Watch out: random assignment within each pair is still a paired design; what matters is that the observations are matched.', 'paired', array['paired t-test', 'paired t test', 'paired t', 'matched pairs', 'matched pair', 'matched pairs t-test', 'paired test']::text[], null, 'paired design matched pairs twins t test'),
  ('stats-inference/two-prop-se', 'stats-inference', 65, '$\hat{p}_1 = 0.5$ with $n_1 = 100$, and $\hat{p}_2 = 0.4$ with $n_2 = 100$. What is the unpooled standard error of $\hat{p}_1 - \hat{p}_2$?', '$0.07$.

$\mathrm{SE} = \sqrt{\dfrac{0.5 \times 0.5}{100} + \dfrac{0.4 \times 0.6}{100}} = \sqrt{0.0025 + 0.0024} = \sqrt{0.0049} = 0.07$.

Tip: this is the **standard error** used for a confidence interval for $p_1 - p_2$.', '0.07', array['7/100']::text[], null, 'standard error difference of two proportions confidence interval'),
  ('stats-inference/two-prop-ci-zero', 'stats-inference', 66, 'A $95\%$ CI for $p_1 - p_2$ is $0.10 \pm 1.96(0.07) = (-0.037,\ 0.237)$. Does it give convincing evidence that the proportions differ? Answer yes or no.', 'No.

The interval includes $0$, so $p_1 = p_2$ is plausible at the $95\%$ level, even though $\hat{p}_1 > \hat{p}_2$ in the sample.

Watch out: a sample difference of $0.10$ is not evidence of a population difference when the interval is that wide.', 'no', array['n', 'not convincing']::text[], null, 'two proportion confidence interval includes zero evidence of difference'),
  ('stats-inference/anova-hypotheses', 'stats-inference', 67, 'What are the hypotheses of a one-way ANOVA comparing $k$ group means?', '$H_0$: all $k$ population means are equal. $H_a$: at least one mean differs.

ANOVA tests the **null hypothesis** $\mu_1 = \mu_2 = \cdots = \mu_k$ with a single $F$ test.

Watch out: $H_a$ does not say that all means differ, only that they are not all equal.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'one-way ANOVA null alternative hypotheses all means equal'),
  ('stats-inference/anova-vs-many-t-tests', 'stats-inference', 68, 'Why run one ANOVA instead of many two-sample $t$ tests when comparing several means?', 'Many separate tests inflate the overall Type I error rate.

With $k = 4$ groups there are $6$ pairs; at $\alpha = 0.05$ each, the chance of at least one false positive is far above $5\%$. One **$F$ test** keeps the overall error rate at $\alpha$.

Tip: if the $F$ test is significant, follow up with a post hoc procedure to see which means differ.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ANOVA versus multiple t tests type I error inflation'),
  ('stats-inference/f-statistic-ratio', 'stats-inference', 69, 'What ratio defines the $F$ statistic in a one-way ANOVA?', '$F = \dfrac{\text{variation between group means}}{\text{variation within groups}} = \dfrac{\mathrm{MSG}}{\mathrm{MSE}}$

Each **mean square** is a sum of squares divided by its degrees of freedom.

Key idea: if $H_0$ is true, both mean squares estimate the same variance and $F$ should be near $1$.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'F statistic ANOVA between over within group variation'),
  ('stats-inference/anova-df-numerator', 'stats-inference', 70, 'A one-way ANOVA compares $4$ groups with $40$ observations in total. How many numerator (between-group) degrees of freedom?', '$3$.

$\mathrm{df}_{\text{between}} = k - 1 = 4 - 1 = 3$.

Tip: the denominator degrees of freedom are $N - k = 40 - 4 = 36$.', '3', array['three']::text[], null, 'ANOVA numerator degrees of freedom k minus 1'),
  ('stats-inference/anova-df-denominator', 'stats-inference', 71, 'A one-way ANOVA compares $4$ groups with $40$ observations in total. How many denominator (within-group) degrees of freedom?', '$36$.

$\mathrm{df}_{\text{within}} = N - k = 40 - 4 = 36$.

Watch out: the two degrees of freedom are $(k - 1,\ N - k) = (3, 36)$; swapping them gives the wrong $F$ table entry.', '36', '{}'::text[], null, 'ANOVA denominator degrees of freedom N minus k'),
  ('stats-inference/f-large-meaning', 'stats-inference', 72, 'In a one-way ANOVA, what does a large $F$ statistic (and a small p-value) suggest?', 'The group means differ more than the within-group variation would explain.

The p-value is the **upper-tail** area of the $F$ distribution, because only large values of $F$ are evidence against $H_0$.

Watch out: a significant $F$ says that some means differ, not which ones.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'large F statistic small p-value ANOVA interpretation upper tail'),
  ('stats-inference/anova-conditions', 'stats-inference', 73, 'What conditions should hold for a one-way ANOVA $F$ test?', 'Independent random samples, roughly normal groups, and similar spreads.

- **Independent**: random samples or random assignment, with independent observations.
- **Normal**: each group is roughly normal, or the samples are large.
- **Equal variability**: the group standard deviations are similar (a common check: the largest is less than twice the smallest).

Tip: ANOVA is fairly robust to mild departures from normality when the group sizes are equal.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ANOVA conditions independence normality equal variances'),
  ('stats-inference/anova-post-hoc', 'stats-inference', 74, 'After a significant ANOVA $F$ test, how do you find which group means differ?', 'Use a post hoc multiple-comparison procedure, such as Tukey''s HSD.

These procedures compare pairs of means while controlling the **overall** error rate across all the comparisons.

Watch out: running ordinary two-sample $t$ tests on every pair brings back the inflated Type I error that ANOVA was meant to avoid.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ANOVA post hoc test Tukey HSD which means differ'),
  ('stats-inference/f-equals-t-squared', 'stats-inference', 75, 'A two-group ANOVA and a pooled two-sample $t$ test are run on the same data, with $t = 3$. What is $F$?', '$9$.

With two groups, $F = t^2 = 3^2 = 9$, and the p-values are identical.

Key idea: ANOVA with two groups is the **pooled** two-sample $t$ test in disguise.', '9', array['nine']::text[], null, 'ANOVA two groups F equals t squared pooled t test'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-gof-df', 'stats-inference', 76, 'A chi-square goodness-of-fit test compares data across $6$ categories. How many degrees of freedom?', '$5$.

$\mathrm{df} = (\text{number of categories}) - 1 = 6 - 1 = 5$.

Watch out: for a test of independence, use $(r-1)(c-1)$ instead.', '5', array['five']::text[], null, 'chi-square goodness of fit degrees of freedom categories minus 1'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-gof-expected', 'stats-inference', 77, 'A die is rolled $120$ times. Under the hypothesis that it is fair, what is the expected count for each face?', '$20$.

$E = n \times p_0 = 120 \times \dfrac{1}{6} = 20$.

Tip: expected counts come from the **null hypothesis**, not from the data, so they need not be whole numbers.', '20', array['twenty']::text[], null, 'chi-square goodness of fit expected count fair die'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-statistic-formula', 'stats-inference', 78, 'What is the formula for the chi-square test statistic?', '$\chi^2 = \sum \dfrac{(O - E)^2}{E}$, summed over every category or cell.

Here $O$ is the **observed** count and $E$ the expected count under $H_0$. Each term measures how far one cell is from expectation.

Watch out: use counts, never percentages or proportions, in the formula.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'chi-square test statistic formula observed expected counts'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-cell-contribution', 'stats-inference', 79, 'A cell has observed count $O = 30$ and expected count $E = 20$. What is its contribution $\dfrac{(O-E)^2}{E}$ to $\chi^2$?', '$5$.

$\dfrac{(30 - 20)^2}{20} = \dfrac{100}{20} = 5$.

Tip: the cells with the largest contributions are where the data depart most from $H_0$.', '5', array['five']::text[], null, 'chi-square cell contribution observed minus expected squared over expected'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-expected-independence', 'stats-inference', 80, 'In a test of independence, a cell has row total $60$ and column total $50$, and the table total is $200$. What is the expected count for that cell?', '$15$.

$E = \dfrac{\text{row total} \times \text{column total}}{\text{table total}} = \dfrac{60 \times 50}{200} = 15$.

Key idea: if the variables are independent, the cell''s share of the table equals the product of its row share and column share.', '15', array['fifteen']::text[], null, 'chi-square independence expected count row total times column total'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-conditions', 'stats-inference', 81, 'What conditions should hold for a chi-square test?', 'Random data, independent observations, and all expected counts at least $5$.

Check the **expected** counts, not the observed ones. If some are too small, combine categories or use an exact test.

Watch out: the observations must be counts of individuals, each in exactly one category.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'chi-square test conditions expected counts at least 5'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-independence-hypotheses', 'stats-inference', 82, 'What are $H_0$ and $H_a$ in a chi-square test of independence?', '$H_0$: the two categorical variables are independent. $H_a$: they are associated.

Independence means that knowing one variable tells you nothing about the **distribution** of the other.

Watch out: a significant result shows association, not causation, and not its strength or direction.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'chi-square test of independence null alternative hypotheses association'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-homogeneity-vs-independence', 'stats-inference', 83, 'How does a chi-square test of homogeneity differ from a test of independence?', 'Homogeneity compares the distribution of one categorical variable across several populations; independence tests two variables within one sample.

The **calculations are identical**; what differs is the sampling design and the wording of the hypotheses.

Example: surveying three separately sampled schools about lunch preferences is homogeneity; sampling one school and recording grade level and preference is independence.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'chi-square test of homogeneity versus independence difference'),
  ('stats-inference/chi-upper-tail-only', 'stats-inference', 84, 'Why is the p-value of a chi-square test found from the upper tail only?', 'Because $\chi^2 \ge 0$ and any departure from $H_0$, in either direction, makes it larger.

Only large values of $\chi^2$ are evidence against $H_0$. A value near $0$ means the data match the expected counts closely.

Watch out: the **chi-square** distribution is right-skewed, so there is no left tail to double.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'chi-square test p-value upper tail right-skewed large values'),
  ('stats-inference/scatterplot-features', 'stats-inference', 85, 'Which four features should you describe when interpreting a scatterplot?', 'Direction, form, strength and unusual features.

- **Direction**: positive or negative association.
- **Form**: linear or curved.
- **Strength**: how closely the points follow the pattern.
- **Unusual features**: outliers, clusters, gaps.

Tip: always look at the scatterplot before computing $r$, since $r$ only makes sense for linear patterns.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'scatterplot describe direction form strength unusual features'),
  ('stats-inference/r-from-r-squared', 'stats-inference', 86, 'A least-squares line with a positive slope has $R^2 = 0.49$. What is $r$?', '$0.7$.

$r = \pm\sqrt{R^2} = \pm 0.7$, and the sign matches the slope, so $r = +0.7$.

Watch out: $R^2$ alone cannot tell you the **direction** of the association; the sign of the slope does.', '0.7', array['7/10']::text[], null, 'r from r squared sign of slope correlation coefficient'),
  ('stats-inference/regression-prediction', 'stats-inference', 87, 'The least-squares line is $\hat{y} = 20 + 3x$. What is the predicted $y$ when $x = 12$?', '$56$.

$\hat{y} = 20 + 3(12) = 20 + 36 = 56$.

Tip: a prediction is an average for all individuals with that $x$, not a guarantee for any one individual.', '56', '{}'::text[], null, 'regression line prediction substitute x value predicted y'),
  ('stats-inference/extrapolation', 'stats-inference', 88, 'Why is it risky to use a regression line to predict $y$ for an $x$ far outside the range of the data?', 'The linear pattern may not continue beyond the observed range.

This is **extrapolation**. A line fitted to heights of children, for example, would predict absurd heights at age $40$.

Watch out: a high $R^2$ does not make extrapolated predictions trustworthy.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'extrapolation regression risky predict outside range of data'),
  ('stats-inference/interpret-slope-context', 'stats-inference', 89, 'A regression of exam score ($y$, points) on hours studied ($x$) gives $\hat{y} = 25 + 4.5x$. How do you interpret the slope?', 'Each additional hour of study is associated with an increase of $4.5$ points in the predicted score, on average.

Always include the **units**, the words "predicted" or "on average", and the context.

Watch out: do not say that studying one more hour causes a $4.5$-point gain unless the data come from a randomized experiment.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'interpret slope regression line context units predicted change'),
  ('stats-inference/residual-plot-pattern', 'stats-inference', 90, 'What does a curved pattern in a residual plot tell you about a linear model?', 'A straight line is not an appropriate model: the true relationship is curved.

A good fit shows a **random scatter** of residuals around $0$ with no pattern. A curve means the form is wrong, and a funnel shape means the spread changes with $x$.

Tip: try a transformation (such as $\log y$) or a curved model.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'residual plot curved pattern linear model not appropriate'),
  ('stats-inference/residuals-sum', 'stats-inference', 91, 'For a least-squares line that includes an intercept, what is the sum of all the residuals?', '$0$.

The least-squares line balances the points above and below it, so the positive and negative **residuals** cancel exactly.

Watch out: a sum of $0$ does not mean the fit is good; it holds for every least-squares line, good or bad.', '0', array['zero']::text[], null, 'sum of residuals least squares line equals zero'),
  ('stats-inference/influential-point', 'stats-inference', 92, 'What is an influential point in regression, and how can you spot one?', 'A point whose removal noticeably changes the slope or intercept.

Points far from $\bar{x}$ in the $x$ direction have **high leverage** and often pull the line toward them. Refit the line with and without the point to check.

Watch out: an outlier in $y$ near the middle of the $x$ values changes the intercept but barely changes the slope.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'influential point high leverage regression outlier effect on slope'),
  ('stats-inference/slope-t-statistic', 'stats-inference', 93, 'A regression slope has $b_1 = 2.4$ with standard error $0.8$. What is the $t$ statistic for $H_0: \beta_1 = 0$?', '$3$.

$t = \dfrac{b_1 - 0}{\mathrm{SE}_{b_1}} = \dfrac{2.4}{0.8} = 3$.

Tip: $H_0: \beta_1 = 0$ says that there is no **linear** relationship between $x$ and $y$ in the population.', '3', array['three']::text[], null, 't statistic regression slope test beta 1 zero'),
  ('stats-inference/slope-df', 'stats-inference', 94, 'A regression of $y$ on $x$ uses $n = 27$ data points. How many degrees of freedom does the $t$ test for the slope have?', '$25$.

$\mathrm{df} = n - 2 = 27 - 2 = 25$.

Why: two parameters, the slope and the intercept, are estimated from the data, so two degrees of freedom are lost.', '25', '{}'::text[], null, 'regression slope t test degrees of freedom n minus 2'),
  ('stats-inference/regression-conditions', 'stats-inference', 95, 'What does the LINE acronym stand for in the conditions for regression inference?', 'Linear, Independent, Normal, Equal variance.

- **Linear**: the true relationship is linear (check the residual plot).
- **Independent**: the observations are independent.
- **Normal**: the residuals are roughly normal for each $x$.
- **Equal variance**: the spread of the residuals is the same at every $x$.

Mnemonic: LINE, the four conditions for a line to be a good model for inference.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'LINE conditions regression inference linear independent normal equal variance'),
  ('stats-inference/residual-standard-deviation', 'stats-inference', 96, 'A regression has $\mathrm{SSE} = 100$ (the sum of squared residuals) and $n = 27$. What is the residual standard deviation $s_e = \sqrt{\mathrm{SSE}/(n-2)}$?', '$2$.

$s_e = \sqrt{\dfrac{100}{27 - 2}} = \sqrt{4} = 2$.

Tip: $s_e$ estimates the typical size of a prediction error, in the units of $y$.', '2', array['two']::text[], null, 'residual standard deviation regression SSE n minus 2'),
  ('stats-inference/r-squared-from-sums', 'stats-inference', 97, 'A regression has total variation $\mathrm{SST} = 200$ and unexplained variation $\mathrm{SSE} = 50$. What is $R^2$?', '$0.75$.

$R^2 = 1 - \dfrac{\mathrm{SSE}}{\mathrm{SST}} = 1 - \dfrac{50}{200} = 0.75$.

Key idea: $R^2$ is the fraction of the total variation in $y$ that the model explains, since $\mathrm{SST} = \mathrm{SSR} + \mathrm{SSE}$.', '0.75', array['3/4', '75%']::text[], null, 'R squared from SSE and SST regression explained variation'),
  ('stats-inference/slope-ci-lower', 'stats-inference', 98, 'A regression has $b_1 = 1.5$ and $\mathrm{SE}_{b_1} = 0.5$. Using $t^* = 2.0$, what is the lower endpoint of the confidence interval for the true slope?', '$0.5$.

$b_1 \pm t^*\,\mathrm{SE}_{b_1} = 1.5 \pm 2.0(0.5) = 1.5 \pm 1.0 = (0.5,\ 2.5)$.

Tip: because the interval excludes $0$, the test of $H_0: \beta_1 = 0$ would reject at the matching level.', '0.5', array['1/2']::text[], null, 'confidence interval for regression slope worked example t critical value'),
  ('stats-inference/multiple-regression-slope', 'stats-inference', 99, 'In a multiple regression of $y$ on $x_1$ and $x_2$, how do you interpret the coefficient of $x_1$?', 'It is the predicted change in $y$ for a $1$-unit increase in $x_1$, **holding $x_2$ constant**.

Because the other variable is held fixed, the coefficient of $x_1$ can differ from the slope in a simple regression of $y$ on $x_1$ alone.

Watch out: adding variables never lowers $R^2$; use adjusted $R^2$ to compare models of different sizes.', null, '{}'::text[], null, 'multiple regression coefficient interpretation holding other variables constant');

-- Students' copies they never edited follow the new text.
update public.memories m
   set title = r.title, body = r.body, answer = r.answer,
       accepted_answers = r.accepted_answers, answer_display = r.answer_display, search_query = r.search_query
  from pack_card_rows r
  join public.pack_cards c on c.id = r.id
 where m.pack_card_id = r.id
   and m.title = c.title and m.body = c.body
   and m.answer is not distinct from c.answer and m.accepted_answers = c.accepted_answers
   and (m.title, m.body, m.answer, m.accepted_answers, m.answer_display, m.search_query)
       is distinct from (r.title, r.body, r.answer, r.accepted_answers, r.answer_display, r.search_query);

insert into public.pack_cards (id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers, answer_display, search_query)
select id, pack_id, position, title, body, answer, accepted_answers, answer_display, search_query from pack_card_rows
on conflict (id) do update set pack_id = excluded.pack_id, position = excluded.position, title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body, answer = excluded.answer, accepted_answers = excluded.accepted_answers, answer_display = excluded.answer_display, search_query = excluded.search_query;

-- Prune cards and packs that were removed from the content.
delete from public.pack_cards c where not exists (select 1 from pack_card_rows r where r.id = c.id);
delete from public.packs where id not in ('calc-limits', 'calc-derivatives', 'calc-derivative-apps', 'calc-integration', 'calc-series', 'calc-multivariable', 'trig-unit-circle', 'trig-identities', 'trig-triangles', 'trig-graphs', 'linalg-systems-matrices', 'linalg-spaces-eigen', 'diffeq-first-order', 'diffeq-higher-order-systems', 'bio-cell-structure', 'bio-cell-division', 'bio-molecular', 'bio-genetics', 'bio-metabolism', 'bio-evolution', 'bio-physiology', 'ap-tissues-skeleton-muscle', 'ap-organ-systems', 'micro-microbes', 'micro-immunology', 'eng-circuits', 'eng-statics', 'eng-materials', 'eng-thermo', 'eng-fluids', 'eng-digital-logic', 'sw-data-structures', 'sw-algorithms', 'sw-git', 'sw-sql', 'sw-web', 'sw-oop', 'sw-os', 'ml-foundations', 'ml-deep-learning', 'discrete-logic-proofs', 'discrete-counting-graphs', 'chem-atoms-bonding', 'chem-stoich-gases', 'chem-equilibrium', 'chem-thermo-kinetics', 'chem-organic', 'orgo-structure-stereo', 'orgo-reactions-mechanisms', 'biochem-molecules-enzymes', 'biochem-metabolism', 'phys-kinematics', 'phys-energy-momentum', 'phys-em', 'phys-waves-modern', 'stats-descriptive', 'stats-probability', 'stats-distributions', 'stats-inference');
delete from public.pack_categories where id not in ('calculus', 'trigonometry', 'linear-algebra', 'differential-equations', 'biology', 'anatomy-physiology', 'microbiology', 'engineering', 'software', 'machine-learning', 'discrete-math', 'chemistry', 'organic-chemistry', 'biochemistry', 'physics', 'statistics');

drop table pack_card_rows;
